window.docContents[93].resolve({"/Logic/#Logic":{"contents":"\n\nUnlike earlier chapters, there are probably too many\nWORKINCLASSes in this chapter.  BCP 20: But conversely some more\nquizzes would be great!\n\nimport LF.Basics\nimport LF.Induction\nimport LF.Poly\nimport LF.Tactics\nimport LF.CustomTactics\n\n\nvariable (a b c : Prop) (n m : Nat) (α : Type) (e1 e2 x y : α)\n\n\n\n\nSo far, we have seen:* propositions: mathematical statements, so far only of 3 kinds:* equality propositions (e1 = e2)* implications (a -> b)* quantified propositions (∀ x, a)* proofs: ways of presenting evidence for the truth of a\n   propositionIn this chapter we will introduce several more flavors of both\npropositions and proofs.Like everything in Lean, well-formed propositions have a type:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations","header":"8. Logic in Lean","id":"/Logic/#Logic"}});